    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "ETFs are traded on exchanges throughout the day like stocks, allowing investors to buy or sell at the current market price whenever trading is open. In contrast, mutual funds are priced only once per day after the market closes based on the average value of the underlying assets. This key difference means ETFs offer superior intraday liquidity and the ability to execute strategies like short selling, whereas mutual fund purchases typically involve waiting until the next day for confirmation at the established end-of-day price.",
