    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. can u also compare the management styles? like, how do the fees and the way the portfolios are actually run (active vs passive) usually differ between the two?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "While both ETFs and mutual funds can be either actively or passively managed, actively managed mutual funds typically command higher expense ratios due to the significant costs of research and portfolio management. Conversely, the ETF structure often naturally incentivizes lower-cost passively managed options that track specific indexes, though active ETFs are also available with varying fee structures. Additionally, because ETFs are traded like stocks, the transaction price can sometimes deviate from their Net Asset Value (NAV), whereas mutual fund transactions are always executed at the precise end-of-day NAV, which can affect how investors perceive cost from a trading experience standpoint. In terms of fees specifically, you often find lower fees associated with many ETF providers, although premium funds across both categories exist.",
      "source": "target",
